class.description:
Mermaid flowcharts render on GitHub and in many tools, and they diff line by line. [FlowGraphMermaid] writes the structure of a [FlowGraph] as plain Mermaid and keeps everything else in [code]%% flow:[/code] comment lines, which Mermaid ignores. The round trip is lossless: node types, properties, ports, connections, frames, variables and editor data come back unchanged. Files with the extension [code].mmd[/code] load and save as [FlowGraph] through [ResourceLoader] and [ResourceSaver].
[b]Mapping:[/b]
- A node with id [code]3[/code] is [code]n3["Title"][/code] (start and end nodes as stadium [code]n3(["Title"])[/code], branch nodes as rhombus [code]n3{"Title"}[/code]), followed by [code]%% flow:node n3 {...}[/code] with its type and stored properties as JSON (the tree of the JSON format of [ResourceTextSerializer]).
- A flow connection is [code]n3 -->|out| n5[/code]. The label is the output port; [code]out:enter[/code] if the input port is not [code]in[/code].
- A data connection is the dotted [code]n4 -.->|value:condition| n1[/code], or [code]%% flow:edge n4 value n1 condition[/code] if [member FlowGraphMermaidOptions.draw_data_connections] is [code]false[/code].
- A frame is [code]subgraph f0["Title"][/code] listing its nodes, then [code]end[/code] and [code]%% flow:frame f0 {...}[/code].
- The properties of the graph are in [code]%% flow:graph {...}[/code].
[b]Hand-written flowcharts:[/b] a node without [code]%% flow:node[/code] line gets its type from its shape and edges: a rhombus [code]{...}[/code] is a [FlowBranchNode], a node without incoming flow edge a [FlowStartNode] (the first one becomes [member FlowGraph.start_node_id]), a node without outgoing flow edge a [FlowEndNode], any other a [FlowEmitEventNode] with the label as [member FlowEmitEventNode.event_name]. The label becomes [member FlowNode.title_override]. Ids other than [code]n<number>[/code] get new ids in order of appearance. An edge label that names an output port of the source uses it; any other label (such as [code]yes[/code]/[code]no[/code]) takes the next unused flow output, so the first edge of a branch leaves [code]true[/code] and the second [code]false[/code]. Dotted (data) edges need a label [code]output:input[/code]. If no node of the chart has a [code]%% flow:node[/code] line, the nodes are placed in layers along the edges, in the direction of the header ([code]TD[/code], [code]LR[/code], [code]BT[/code], [code]RL[/code]), so the graph opens readable in the editor.
[b]Supported subset:[/b] the header [code]flowchart[/code] or [code]graph[/code] (any direction), ids of letters, digits and [code]_[/code], node shapes with quoted or plain labels, edges [code]-->[/code], [code]---[/code], [code]==>[/code], [code]-.->[/code], [code]-.-[/code] with [code]|label|[/code] or [code]-- label -->[/code], chains such as [code]a --> b --> c[/code], [code]subgraph[/code]/[code]end[/code] and a trailing [code];[/code]. Ignored and lost on load: [code]classDef[/code], [code]class[/code], [code]style[/code], [code]linkStyle[/code], [code]click[/code], [code]direction[/code] inside subgraphs, [code]:::class[/code] suffixes, other comments and hand formatting (saving rewrites the file). Errors with line and message: other diagram types, [code]&[/code] groups, edge ids, arrow heads [code]x[/code]/[code]o[/code].
Errors are kept per instance, so graphs can be read on several threads at once.
[codeblocks]
[gdscript]
var text := FlowGraphMermaid.serialize(graph)

var mermaid := FlowGraphMermaid.new()
if mermaid.parse(text) == OK:
	var loaded := mermaid.get_graph()
else:
	print("Line ", mermaid.get_error_line(), ": ", mermaid.get_error_message())
[/gdscript]
[csharp]
string text = FlowGraphMermaid.Serialize(graph);

var mermaid = new FlowGraphMermaid();
if (mermaid.Parse(text) == Error.Ok)
{
    FlowGraph loaded = mermaid.GetGraph();
}
else
{
    GD.Print("Line ", mermaid.GetErrorLine(), ": ", mermaid.GetErrorMessage());
}
[/csharp]
[/codeblocks]
